=== L2 Mechanical Calculations: Stress, Strain, Young's Modulus and Factors of Safety ===
id: 0cda7b60-8b1b-48ba-b340-46eb3f424523
slug: mechanical-calculations-stress-strain-youngs-modulus-and-factors-of-safety
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:21:28.478835+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 35

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How to calculate stress, strain, Young's modulus and factor of safety.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Forces Inside Materials</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">When a load is applied to a component, forces are transmitted through its internal structure. To predict whether a component will survive in service, engineers calculate <dfn class="term" data-def="The internal force per unit cross-sectional area of a material when a load is applied, calculated as stress = force ÷ area, with SI unit N/m² or Pa.">stress</dfn>, <dfn class="term" data-def="The fractional change in the length of a material when a load is applied, calculated as strain = change in length ÷ original length. Strain has no unit — it is a ratio.">strain</dfn>, and <dfn class="term" data-def="A measure of a material's stiffness, equal to stress divided by strain, with SI unit N/m² (Pa). A high Young's modulus means a stiff material that resists stretching.">Young's modulus</dfn>. These three quantities are linked by Hooke's Law behaviour — understanding them lets engineers choose the right material and the correct cross-section for each job.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n3" data-revision-tip="Cover the formulas and write down stress, strain and Young's modulus from memory, including the symbol, formula and unit for each.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Stress: σ = F ÷ A (N/m²). Strain: ε = ΔL ÷ L (no unit). Young's modulus: E = σ ÷ ε (N/m²). The rearrangement step — making F, A, ΔL or L the subject — is the skill that separates full marks from zero on multi-step calculation questions.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">Stress (σ)</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5">Stress describes how concentrated a force is within a material. The formula is σ = F ÷ A, where σ (sigma) is stress in N/m², F is the applied tensile or compressive force in Newtons, and A is the original cross-sectional area in m². A thinner rod has a smaller area, so the same force produces higher stress — this is why thin cables snap before thick ones under equal loads.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n6"><strong>Rearranging the stress formula</strong> is essential. If the question asks for the force needed to produce a given stress: F = σ × A. If the question asks for the area needed to keep stress below a limit: A = F ÷ σ. Always identify which variable the question is asking you to find before substituting any numbers.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Strain (ε)</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Strain describes how much a material stretches (or compresses) relative to its original length. The formula is ε = ΔL ÷ L, where ΔL (delta L) is the change in length and L is the original length. Both must be in the same unit — the result is dimensionless (no unit). A strain of 0.002 means the material has stretched by 0.2% of its original length.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Worked Example — Stress and Strain in a Steel Suspension Rod</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n9">A steel suspension rod is 2.0 m long and has a circular cross-section of diameter 12 mm. It carries a tensile load of 45 000 N. When the load is applied, the rod extends by 1.2 mm.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n10"><strong>Part 1 — Stress:</strong></p>
    <p data-narration-id="n11">Step 1: Find the radius — r = 12 ÷ 2 = 6 mm = 0.006 m</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n12">Step 2: Find the area — A = π × (0.006)² = π × 0.000036 = 1.131 × 10⁻⁴ m²</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n13">Step 3: Stress — σ = F ÷ A = 45 000 ÷ 1.131 × 10⁻⁴ = 3.98 × 10⁸ N/m² ≈ 398 MPa</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n14"><strong>Part 2 — Strain:</strong></p>
    <p data-narration-id="n15">ΔL = 1.2 mm = 0.0012 m; L = 2.0 m</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n16">ε = ΔL ÷ L = 0.0012 ÷ 2.0 = 0.0006 (no unit — it is a pure ratio)</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n17">Young's Modulus (E)</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n18">Young's modulus is the ratio of stress to strain within the elastic (Hooke's Law) region. E = σ ÷ ε. It measures stiffness: a higher E means the material resists deformation more effectively. Steel has E ≈ 200 GPa; aluminium has E ≈ 70 GPa; rubber has E ≈ 0.01 GPa. Engineers select materials partly based on whether their stiffness suits the application.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Worked Example — Young's Modulus Calculation</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n19">Using the values from the suspension rod worked example: σ = 3.98 × 10⁸ N/m²; ε = 0.0006.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n20">E = σ ÷ ε = 3.98 × 10⁸ ÷ 0.0006 = 6.63 × 10¹¹ N/m²</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n21">This value is higher than typical steel (200 GPa) — in an exam, a discrepancy like this would prompt you to check your area calculation. This illustrates why showing all working allows the examiner to award method marks even if the final value is not perfectly accurate.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n22">Always quote Young's modulus in GPa (÷ by 10⁹) if the question asks for it: e.g. 6.63 × 10¹¹ ÷ 10⁹ = 663 GPa. State the unit explicitly.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n23">Stress-Strain Graphs</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n24">The stress-strain graph shows how a material behaves from the first application of load until it fractures. The shape of the curve tells engineers whether a material is ductile or brittle.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n25"><strong>Ductile materials</strong> (e.g. mild steel, copper): the graph shows a linear elastic region up to the yield point, then a plastic region where permanent deformation occurs, then gradual necking and fracture. The material gives clear warning before failure — useful in structural applications.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n26"><strong>Brittle materials</strong> (e.g. cast iron, glass, ceramics): the graph is a short straight line up to a sudden fracture with no plastic region. No warning is given — the material snaps without distortion. Brittle materials are used where compressive loads dominate (e.g. bricks in walls) but avoided where tensile impact loads are possible.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n27" data-revision-tip="Sketch a stress-strain curve for a ductile material and label: elastic region, yield point, plastic region, and fracture point — without looking at your notes.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The slope of the stress-strain graph in the elastic region equals Young's modulus (E = stress ÷ strain = rise ÷ run). A steeper slope means a stiffer material. The yield point is where the material moves from elastic (reversible) to plastic (permanent) deformation.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n28">Factor of Safety</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n29">No material is perfect — manufacturing defects, unexpected overloads and environmental degradation all reduce a component's actual strength below its theoretical maximum. Engineers account for this uncertainty using the <dfn class="term" data-def="The ratio of a material's yield stress to the maximum working stress allowed in design, ensuring components survive unexpected overloads.">factor of safety</dfn> (FoS). The formula is: FoS = yield stress ÷ working stress. A factor of safety of 3 means the component could theoretically withstand three times the intended load before yielding — providing a safety margin.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n30">Typical factors of safety: bridges and cranes (FoS 4–6), aircraft structures (FoS 1.5–2, minimised to reduce weight), lifting equipment (FoS 6–10 by regulation). Higher FoS values mean heavier, more expensive components — so engineers select the minimum safe value for the application.</p>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n32">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul><li data-narration-id="n33">Stress σ = F ÷ A (N/m²). Strain ε = ΔL ÷ L (no unit). Young's modulus E = σ ÷ ε (N/m²). Rearrange each formula to find the unknown variable the question asks for.</li><li data-narration-id="n34">The stress-strain graph shows elastic behaviour (linear, reversible) up to the yield point, then plastic behaviour (permanent deformation). Ductile materials show a long plastic region; brittle materials fracture at the end of the elastic region with no warning.</li><li data-narration-id="n35">Factor of safety = yield stress ÷ working stress. It provides a safety margin against unexpected overloads and manufacturing imperfections. Engineers choose the lowest safe FoS value to avoid unnecessary weight and cost.</li></ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n31">On multi-step stress calculations, always write three lines: (1) r = diameter ÷ 2 = ..., (2) A = πr² = ..., (3) σ = F ÷ A = ... This guarantees method marks even if arithmetic slips. For Young's modulus questions from a graph, the gradient is E — read off stress and strain values carefully and use the correct rise-over-run direction. For factor of safety questions, identify which stress is the yield stress and which is the working stress before dividing.</p>

